body:
> We should report every case where an unused assignment happens. (At the moment, we only report it in one place -- where the variable was declared.)

:-1: That will be very noisy and I don't think it adds much to the user experience. This rule is not about assignments, it's about identifiers that were defined and then never used. We do not consider an assignment only to be used, so this should warn:

@mysticatea you removed "accepted" from this but didn't indicate why. Can you explain?

@nzakas I see your point about which line to report. However, I still think we should improve the error in these cases (even if just by improving the error message when a variable is assigned but never read, like what happens in the following case):

``` js
var foo;

// other logic

foo = 5;
```

This has led to a lot of confusion for users (see https://github.com/eslint/eslint/issues/3417, https://github.com/eslint/eslint/issues/3129, https://github.com/eslint/eslint/issues/3719, https://github.com/eslint/eslint/issues/3917, https://github.com/eslint/eslint/issues/4830, https://github.com/eslint/eslint/issues/5177, https://github.com/eslint/eslint/issues/5727, https://github.com/eslint/eslint/issues/5942, https://github.com/eslint/eslint/issues/6243, https://github.com/eslint/eslint/issues/7027, https://github.com/eslint/eslint/issues/7107, https://github.com/eslint/eslint/issues/7286). The confusion is that users think `foo` is used because it's assigned to, even though the assignment is unnecessary. The rule is certainly correct in reporting these cases, but maybe we should make it more obvious that the rule took the assignment into account.
